! D.frr — Tier-2 regional transit AS
!
! Relationships (Gao-Rexford):
!   Providers: A (10.0.4.1), B (10.0.5.1)
!   Peer:      E (10.0.7.2)
!   Customers: L (10.1.7.1), M (10.1.8.1)
!
! D buys transit from two tier-1s (A and B) for redundancy and reaches the rest
! of the internet through them. D peers with E at the regional level (settlement-free).
! D sells transit to stub customers L and M.
!
! Money logic:
!   A and B charge D for transit → D prefers routes learned from its own customers
!   (local-pref 300) over peer routes (200) over provider routes (100). This
!   means D will use its providers only as a last resort, which minimises costs.
!
!   D does NOT re-export routes learned from A/B to E (its peer), and does NOT
!   re-export routes from E to A/B (its providers). Doing so would make D an
!   uncompensated transit between its providers and peer — a losing proposition.
!
!   D exports its own prefix and customer routes upstream (to A, B) and to E,
!   so the rest of the internet can reach D's customers L and M.
!
! Local preference:
!   300 = customer-learned  (free money — prefer routing through paying customers)
!   200 = peer-learned      (settlement-free, use when customer path unavailable)
!   100 = provider-learned  (costs money, use only as last resort)
!
! Export policy (valley-free):
!   → providers: own prefix + customer-learned routes only
!   → peer:      own prefix + customer-learned routes only
!   → customers: full table (they pay for global reachability)

bgp community-list standard CUSTOMER-ROUTES permit 65001:100
!
ip prefix-list OWN-PREFIX seq 5 permit 10.255.4.1/32
!
route-map FROM-CUSTOMER permit 10
 set community 65001:100
 set local-preference 300
!
route-map FROM-PEER permit 10
 set community 65001:200
 set local-preference 200
!
route-map FROM-PROVIDER permit 10
 set community 65001:300
 set local-preference 100
!
! To providers and peers: advertise only own prefix and customer routes.
! Advertising peer or provider routes upward/sideways would make D an unpaid
! transit — violating the valley-free property.
route-map TO-PROVIDER permit 10
 match ip address prefix-list OWN-PREFIX
route-map TO-PROVIDER permit 20
 match community CUSTOMER-ROUTES
route-map TO-PROVIDER deny 30
!
route-map TO-PEER permit 10
 match ip address prefix-list OWN-PREFIX
route-map TO-PEER permit 20
 match community CUSTOMER-ROUTES
route-map TO-PEER deny 30
!
! Customers get the full routing table — they pay for global connectivity.
route-map TO-CUSTOMER permit 10
!
router bgp __ASN__
 address-family ipv4 unicast
  ! Providers (buy transit from them)
  neighbor 10.0.4.1 route-map FROM-PROVIDER in
  neighbor 10.0.4.1 route-map TO-PROVIDER out
  neighbor 10.0.5.1 route-map FROM-PROVIDER in
  neighbor 10.0.5.1 route-map TO-PROVIDER out
  ! Peer (settlement-free regional exchange)
  neighbor 10.0.7.2 route-map FROM-PEER in
  neighbor 10.0.7.2 route-map TO-PEER out
  ! Customers (sell transit to them)
  neighbor 10.1.7.1 route-map FROM-CUSTOMER in
  neighbor 10.1.7.1 route-map TO-CUSTOMER out
  neighbor 10.1.8.1 route-map FROM-CUSTOMER in
  neighbor 10.1.8.1 route-map TO-CUSTOMER out
 exit-address-family
!
